WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.304
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.010
JOHN MURRAY: My
name is John Murray.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.990
I'm a product manager
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.330
And I work on what used to
be called the Cloud Services

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.260
Platform.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.450
We didn't change
the session, but we

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.980
did change the name on Tuesday.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.080
So it's now called Anthos.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.120
And I'm going to talk
a little bit today

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.640
about policy
management for that.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.340
So going to go through
some of the tools

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.352
that we have in the
open source software

00:00:32.352 --> 00:00:33.810
that we're shipping
that allows you

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.930
to manage corporate
policies, governance

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.770
policies, and those sorts
of things on top of Anthos,

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.900
as well as talk about
some of the tooling we've

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.090
built on top of that open
source to make that easier,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.810
particularly when
you're running a hybrid

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.460
or multi-cloud environment.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.250
We've got a lot of
distributed compute out there.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.910
So let's get started.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:01.000
For those who did not see
the announcement on Tuesday,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.920
Anthos is our application
modernization platform

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.770
for the hybrid
multi-cloud world.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.720
And the idea here is that we
have services, a marketplace,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.840
and a control plane that lives
in the cloud here on GCP,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.080
and is a central place for
you to manage all that stuff.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.870
And that fans out to a bunch of
different compute environments

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:27.240
either, say, running on GCP,
running on prem with GKE

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.560
On-Prem, or other clouds.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.650
The basis of that are a
number of building blocks.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:38.660
And so it starts kind of at the
bottom with the infrastructure

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:39.680
operator.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.800
So I've got that
role here as somebody

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.530
working on Google Cloud.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.820
But that could be
infrastructure that's

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.320
in any kind of environment.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.760
It could be another cloud.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:50.600
It could be on prem.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.680
And so that's stuff like
storage and networking, VMs,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.480
and other primitive building
blocks of infrastructure.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.360
That's the foundation of Anthos.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.810
On top of that, we have a
Kubernetes distribution.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.780
So GKE in the cloud,
GKE On-Prem, on prem.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.030
And that allows you to
have a uniform computing

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.570
layer with a uniform API across
all of these environments.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.110
That's kind of the first level
where policies come into play,

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:23.870
and where you can start
controlling that environment.

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.720
And then above that,
one of the other layers

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.450
that we have is Istio,
which is our service

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.750
mesh in open source,
but also is something

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.560
that we manage on GKE, and that
we're building tooling around,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.640
such as the cloud services mesh,
which is in alpha right now.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.310
And that really allows you
to think about your services

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:47.630
at the HTTP L7 layer,
abstract those out,

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.490
and think about
traffic as a graph

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.153
in a microservices environment.

00:02:52.153 --> 00:02:53.570
There's a number
of policies there

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.570
that have to do with how
services talk to one another,

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:01.290
how traffic is routed, and we'll
get into that in a little bit.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.110
There are many other
pieces of Anthos

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:04.778
here that you can
bring to the table.

00:03:04.778 --> 00:03:06.320
I'm going to talk
mostly about these,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.740
but we have things like
CloudRun and Knative

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.520
for running
serverless containers.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.300
Although the servers are still
there, as many of you know.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:24.620
And things like our marketplace,
various other bits and pieces.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.680
In addition to that and talk
today about Anthos Config

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.130
Management.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.910
So that is a way to
knit all of this stuff

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:36.080
together and provide you
with a single administrative

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.190
experience for talking to
all these different platforms

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.890
in a distributed environment.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.510
And the way that
that works is that we

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:49.460
use the Kubernetes native policy
formats, which effectively

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.280
are putting all of
those policies in code

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.730
so that you can analyze them
so that they're repeatable,

00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:01.820
and so that the policies that
you apply to this environment

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:05.510
are auditable,
reproducible, transactional,

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:06.290
and self-healing.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.380
What I mean by that is, if
you put all of this stuff

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.510
in a Git repository,
you get a lot

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.690
of the advantages
of source control.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.279
So you can go and look at
everything in one place.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.520
The changes that you make
to a particular cluster

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.440
or the multi-cluster
environment are

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.900
reproducible across new
clusters that you bring in

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.630
or new service meshes
that you bring in.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.960
A Git repository
is transactional

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.410
so that when you make a
commit, you can roll that back.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:37.580
You can roll it forward.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.790
You know exactly what was
changed by when and by whom.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:42.200
And then the
self-healing aspects

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.410
of this or that we are looking
at these Git repositories,

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.300
bringing down the configuration
to all these different places,

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.910
and then constantly reconciling
that in the local environments

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.270
to make sure that if
things drift out of line,

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.060
that we bring them back in.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.140
And I'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:03.990
So let's talk about the policies
that are available to you.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.230
The idea with Anthos
is for us mainly

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.480
to work off of open source.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.910
So there's a lot of
open source stuff

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.640
that you can do on
these platforms, as well

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:18.230
as a lot of stuff that we can
do inside the infrastructure.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:22.940
So to the degree that we've
needed our customers to operate

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.000
on these platforms, we've gone
into the upstream open source

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.010
and tried to contribute
to some of these things

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:31.420
alongside many other folks
who work on the platforms.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.620
For Kubernetes, that means
things like access control.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.310
So Kubernetes has a role-based
access control system

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:40.340
that allows you to define who's
allowed to do what on any given

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.240
cluster, who can deploy
pods, who can make changes

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.530
to policies.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:46.940
Networking.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:50.690
There is a number of different
networking mechanisms in there.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.060
Things like network
policy, which

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.850
allow you to define routing
between individual pods

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:56.820
on a cluster.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.080
We support that in
GKE as well as on prem

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.500
now with our partners
at Tigera, who

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:07.730
make the container networking
interface for Calico.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.820
And then security and isolation.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:13.730
So you can do things like
set up a pod security

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.660
policy in Kubernetes that
allows you to define what

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.420
a container is allowed to
do in terms of interacting

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.270
with the host environment.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.490
And that creates isolation
through things like namespaces,

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:29.210
as well as isolating the
containers from the underlying

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.360
environment to ensure that they
only have the access that they

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.590
need, and they're not
able to break out of that

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.280
and move across boundaries.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:40.700
On the Istio side, one
of the great policies

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.230
there is traffic routing.

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:44.030
So Istio is something
that you can

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.730
use to think about your
services and the level

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:49.580
of the inbound traffic that
you have, and set up a gateway,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.580
and bring in your
ingress traffic.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.010
And then route that
to microservices

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.200
using things like waiting.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.230
Setting up things
like mutual TLS

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:00.890
so that all of the
different services

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:03.620
within your service
mesh are communicating

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.020
in an encrypted fashion.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.090
You can do things like
service-to-service access

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.060
control.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:10.490
And so if you have
a flat network

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.380
where everything is
talking to one another,

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:13.370
that might be fine.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.980
But it might mean
that an attacker can

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:18.185
compromise one of your
services and go look

00:07:18.185 --> 00:07:20.060
at something that they're
not supposed to be.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:22.530
So service-to-service
access control

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.830
is a way to ensure
that only services that

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:26.690
should be talking
to other services

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.650
are sending traffic around.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.820
And then there's a whole bunch
of extensibility in Istio.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.950
So there's a Mixer
component in there

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.580
that allows people
to bring in plug-ins.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.290
And so you can do things
like plug-in telemetry,

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.650
but you can also do things
like plug-in authorization.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.560
And there's a number
of different things

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.910
in the community that
we support there.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.930
And then when we talk
about the infrastructure,

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.390
we're really talking about
the underlying stuff.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.330
So on GCP, that's stuff
like GCP services.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:02.280
So you may have your
clusters backed by storage

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:04.580
is sitting on a GCS bucket.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:08.330
Or you may have your services
backed by a SQL database,

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.430
like Cloud SQL.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.660
Things like firewalls, secure
images, and even the cluster

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.870
flags that are on your cluster.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:21.260
And so we want all of this stuff
to be able to be declarative.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.270
And what I mean
by that is you can

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.830
show up and tell
us exactly how you

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:26.300
want this environment to look.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.980
You can put
constraints around that

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:29.990
to say these are
the types of things

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:33.650
that we want our developers
or our administrators

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:35.220
to be able to do.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.720
And then enforce
all of that in code,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.169
just like you enforce things
when you build applications.

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:44.570
And so shift some of that
compliance further up

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:46.340
into the infrastructure
pipeline,

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.590
and then give you
the tooling to make

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:49.910
that a lot easier to manage.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.670
So I talked a little bit
about API server flags.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:58.428
That's a really important piece
of security on Kubernetes.

00:08:58.428 --> 00:09:00.220
There's a lot of things
that you can set up

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:03.130
a cluster to do that
are relatively insecure.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:06.490
And so it's not sufficient
just to come in and change

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.642
the configuration on
top of your cluster.

00:09:08.642 --> 00:09:10.600
You often have to think
about the configuration

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.110
of the cluster itself.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:14.890
One are the advantages
we have with GKE On-Prem.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:18.040
and that Kubernetes distribution
is that those clusters

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.510
are built declaratively.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:22.570
And so you're able to
specify a cluster actually

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:26.487
as a Kubernetes and object
with all of those flag set.

00:09:26.487 --> 00:09:28.570
And so you have a reproducible
way of stamping out

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.610
clusters that's secure.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.280
And you can validate that
by looking at the code.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.020
Access control I
mentioned already.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.372
RBAC is, I think, the
best and most widely used

00:09:38.372 --> 00:09:39.830
way of managing
that on Kubernetes,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.330
but there are a
number of other ways

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:42.940
you can manage access control.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.410
Network policy I mentioned.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:47.920
Resource quota Is
a way to set up

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.290
policies about who can do
what in terms of resource

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:55.810
consumption so CPU, memory,
number of processes running,

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:57.090
that sort of thing.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:02.740
And that's a great tool if you
have multi-tenant environments.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.718
We've got a lot of people
sharing one cluster.

00:10:04.718 --> 00:10:06.010
You don't want noisy neighbors.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:07.802
You want to keep people
isolated and ensure

00:10:07.802 --> 00:10:10.450
that they're only using
the resources that

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:12.318
are allocated to them.

00:10:12.318 --> 00:10:14.860
Admission control I'll talk a
little bit about later as well,

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:17.350
but that's a point of
extensibility within Kubernetes

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:20.830
that allows you to look at
the inbound traffic on the API

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:25.200
server and enact custom
rules against it.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.930
So you can do things like
bring your business rules in.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:33.640
If they're not handled by
some of these other built-in

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.360
policies, then you can
enforce them yourself.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.980
And finally, pod
security policies,

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:42.190
which allows you to define
what things are allowed to do

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:44.000
vis-a-vis the host system.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.980
And there's a number of
levers and dials there.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.840
For Istio, the
policies are really

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.800
about defining what
you can do in terms

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.140
of service-to-service routing.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:01.480
So Istio-- what it does
is injects an Envoy proxy

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:04.570
as a sidecar to all of your
services that are running.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.540
And then it uses
that mesh of proxies

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:12.640
to enforce policies, as
well as collect telemetry.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:14.770
And so there's a
number of adapters,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.800
as I said there, that allow
you to do things like push

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.950
your telemetry out to
Prometheus and Grafana,

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:24.310
or Stackdriver on Google Cloud.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:26.890
And then you can do
other things like connect

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:33.020
with the Apigee APIs over
GRPC and enforce policy there.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.830
And those sidecars also are able
to pick up policies directly

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:38.470
from the clusters which
Istio is running on.

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:40.540
So you can do things
like enforce mutual TLS,

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.030
which is a great thing to do.

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:46.190
Means that if somebody is in
and able to sniff your traffic,

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:47.950
it's all encrypted.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:50.200
Service-to-service
authorization allows

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.650
you to do routing and rollouts.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.390
And so you can
shape your traffic.

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:56.530
You can move it.

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.620
You can do blue-green
deployments,

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:03.460
all through a top
level set of rules.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:06.640
And then you've got
fine-grained GRPC controls

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.640
if you're doing GRPCs in
between your services.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.610
So infrastructure.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.850
There's a number
of interesting ways

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:20.390
you can provision your
infrastructure as code.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:23.000
I mentioned GKE On-Prem.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.940
Those clusters,
including the CIDR ranges

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:29.420
that they have on prem, the
API flags that you're setting,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:30.620
that's all declarative.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:32.330
So you can specify that once.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:35.060
You can stamp out clusters
that are all doing things

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:36.872
according to what you'd like.

00:12:36.872 --> 00:12:38.330
It is not a scenario
where you have

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:40.790
to have somebody running
a command line tool

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:44.252
and having multiple developers
doing whatever they want.

00:12:44.252 --> 00:12:45.710
As an administrator,
it's something

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:47.390
you can keep control of.

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:50.330
And that GKE On-Prem
API as well is

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.550
able to control the
underlying machines

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:55.250
and so it kind of
abstracts away that the VMs

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:57.080
and allows you to do
things like rolling

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.490
updates of the infrastructure.

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:04.460
So you can do a node
upgrade, scale a cluster, all

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.820
through a code interface.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:10.640
We're taking some of
that declarative stuff

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:14.320
and bringing it to GCP as well
in a Kubernetes environment.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:19.070
So we have a number of GKE
customers who need to use

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:24.020
the underlying storage
resources-- for example,

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:24.800
on GCP--

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:26.400
from their Kubernetes cluster.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.880
Today, if you've got something
that's backed by a GCP service,

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.910
you've got to go
outside of Kubernetes

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:35.360
and go into the GCP APIs
to do the provisioning

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:37.970
of that infrastructure.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:40.340
We've announced a
GCP Cloud Connector

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.100
in alpha, which is a way
for you to do that directly

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.122
from the Kubernetes API.

00:13:45.122 --> 00:13:47.330
So that doesn't have full
coverage of our API service

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:48.230
today.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:52.460
But what it does allow you to do
is manage some of the resources

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:55.310
I've got listed here so that
you can create those things

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.905
as Kubernetes objects.

00:13:56.905 --> 00:13:59.030
So when you're doing a
deployment of an application

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:01.880
that's backed by,
say, Cloud Spanner,

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.460
you can come in and not only
do the deployment directly

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:06.590
against the Kubernetes
API, but you can say,

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:10.577
I also want a Spanner database
to be deployed alongside this.

00:14:10.577 --> 00:14:12.410
And that's defined as
a Kubernetes resource.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:15.440
We use what's called custom
resource definitions, which

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.030
is extensibility built
into the Kubernetes API.

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:21.990
And that allows you to define
these things at that layer.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:26.330
And so if you're coming into
Anthos and you want to do that,

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:31.580
you can manage all of that as
a set of Kubernetes resources.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:37.030
So that's kind of
the set of things

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:41.710
you're able to do with the stuff
that's shipping with Anthos.

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:44.020
That doesn't necessarily
mean that it's

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:48.610
easy to manage a hybrid
multi-cloud setup.

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:51.370
So I want to talk a little
bit about dealing with scale

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:56.440
how you can scale up from maybe
a small footprint into having

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.590
something that runs across
a large tech organization

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:02.830
where you have multiple teams
of administrators, potentially.

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:05.200
Lots and lots of
developers, all working

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.270
in a fairly fractured
hybrid environment.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:08.770
And some of the
tools we're building

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:12.570
to make that a little
bit easier on folks.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.745
So this is a journey that
I see a lot of people

00:15:15.745 --> 00:15:17.495
go through when they
start with Kubernetes

00:15:17.495 --> 00:15:21.810
and they start GKE, is that when
you're evaluating something,

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:25.980
you've got one operator, one set
of policies, and one cluster.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:28.250
That's step one.

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:30.750
And it's fairly easy then to
just take your policy side

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:34.080
and run a command line tool like
Kubectl that pushes policies

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:35.130
to the cluster.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:37.170
And you can keep those
on your local hard drive.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:41.460
And because it's you and your
cluster, everything's fine.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.360
You understand everything
that's going on.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.170
You're the only person
who has to care.

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:49.470
But quickly it gets a
little bit more complicated.

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:51.180
When you bring in a
team of operators,

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:52.555
when you have
multiple people who

00:15:52.555 --> 00:15:55.080
are responsible for managing
a Kubernetes environment,

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.715
you can't just have
a local set of files.

00:15:57.715 --> 00:16:00.090
And you can't just have people
pushing them, necessarily,

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:01.590
from their command
line, because you

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:03.630
can step on each other's toes.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:07.360
One person might be writing
over somebody else's stuff.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:11.190
And so that's where we
think a repository is

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:13.050
a really useful tool.

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:15.600
Source control mechanisms
were built specifically

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:19.350
for this use case, where people
need to collaborate on code.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.780
And you can do things
like use the tooling

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.090
that's built around
them to do code reviews,

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:27.090
to do static analysis
of that code,

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:30.455
and to understand who did what
when to roll those things back,

00:16:30.455 --> 00:16:31.300
et cetera.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:35.850
So there's been decades
of work on repositories.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:38.895
That's a solved problem
for collaborating on code,

00:16:38.895 --> 00:16:41.020
and we want to use that
for infrastructure as well.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.667
But it gets even
more complicated

00:16:45.667 --> 00:16:48.000
when you have these sort of
multi-platform environments.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.450
So if you're running on
GCP, you're running on prem,

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:52.640
you're running in
another cloud, now you

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:54.710
have to deal not only
with collaborating

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.930
with a bunch of people
in a repository.

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:58.970
You have to deal with
getting that repository

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:02.060
and its declarations out
to all those clusters

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:05.630
so that the policies that you've
written are actually applied.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:08.329
And that's a
non-trivial problem.

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:11.369
You can loop through these
things and push the policies.

00:17:11.369 --> 00:17:13.707
But if that fails, you're
halfway through that.

00:17:13.707 --> 00:17:15.290
And now you've got
one set of policies

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.500
in one place, another set
of policies in another.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:20.420
These are distributed
environments.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.790
So people can come in
locally and make changes.

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:24.839
Even if you do your best
to keep them out of that

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:26.297
and keep them
through this channel,

00:17:26.297 --> 00:17:28.369
it's always possible for
something to go down,

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:30.540
something accidental to happen.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.810
So if you're not aware
of that, or you're not

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:35.750
able to reconcile that,
you may have something

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:38.570
that's out of scope
or out of policy,

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:40.190
and you don't even
know about it.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:42.980
So dealing with the
collaboration aspects

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:45.675
and the distribution
aspects is something

00:17:45.675 --> 00:17:48.050
that we wanted to build tooling
around to help folks out.

00:17:50.756 --> 00:17:53.940
I'll quickly talk
about why you might

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.430
want to have a bunch of
different clusters, why you

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.890
might end up in this scenario.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.410
There are a ton of
reasons, and they often

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:04.950
tend to be driven by the
nature of your business

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.230
or the vertical that you're in.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.180
These are some of the
things that we see.

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:12.420
So obviously, everybody who's
been operating for a while

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:13.890
has legacy software.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:15.330
Might have an ERP system.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:17.100
You might have a
Catalog Database

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.607
that's sitting on prem.

00:18:18.607 --> 00:18:20.190
But you want to do
stuff in the cloud,

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:22.107
so you're building your
new site in the cloud.

00:18:22.107 --> 00:18:24.678
You've got to have compute
in both environments

00:18:24.678 --> 00:18:26.970
because the data gravity or
things that just don't make

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:29.100
sense to lift and shift over.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.123
You may want local execution.

00:18:31.123 --> 00:18:33.540
So it's not always possible
to do everything in the cloud,

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:35.748
and particularly if you're
dealing with tons of data.

00:18:35.748 --> 00:18:39.510
If you've got a factory
floor or a warehouse

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:41.940
with IoT stuff running
around, you probably

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:44.520
don't want to ship up every
little last bit of data

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:45.340
up into the cloud.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:47.940
And you might want
compute running locally.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.060
Jurisdictional issues.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:51.400
So if you're a global
operator, you're

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:55.080
going to have the
need to deal with laws

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.810
all over the place that have
to deal with data sovereignty.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:01.350
Keeping things that are
generated in one place

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.780
stored in that place.

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:04.800
Augmenting services.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:06.480
You may have a bunch of
stuff that's running on prem,

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.190
but you want to take
advantage of some

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:09.990
of the great machine
learning tools

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:11.220
that they have in the cloud.

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.360
And so you've got to
get data up there.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:14.100
IoT.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.290
We're running into a
number of retailers

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:19.800
who run Kubernetes clusters
in every store from things

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:24.930
like point of sale systems or
telemetry from various sensors,

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:26.130
and then bursting.

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:28.380
So you've got things
running on prem,

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:31.680
but you want to be able to
have flexible capacity using

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:32.790
the cloud.

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:35.600
So you may have some--

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:38.510
you may have all of
these things driving you.

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:42.110
And where that leads
you is that you end up

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:43.795
with a fairly
fractured environment,

00:19:43.795 --> 00:19:46.170
and you need some kind of glue
to bring it back together.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:52.190
Not only that, but you've
got to deal with compliance.

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:57.330
So you've got an environment
that's growing in complexity.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:00.510
And then you've got
an outside environment

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:05.650
that has ever-increasing
concerns about governance.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:10.590
So that's things like the
alphabet soup of compliance.

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:14.220
The GDPRs, and the
HIPAAs, and the SOX.

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:17.070
It's things like
government regulations.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:21.130
It's also things like business
processes and security needs.

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:25.230
Those are getting more and more
in terms of the number of them.

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.330
They are getting
more complicated.

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.700
And the complexity
of your environment

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:31.960
is just making that worse
and worse over time.

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:35.400
And so we see that, and we
think an important solution

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:39.710
to that is really thinking
about policy as code.

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:42.330
Taking the various
policies that you have

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:44.040
and turning them
into code so that you

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:46.590
can rein in a little
bit of that complexity,

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.620
and start using compute
to deal with it.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:54.430
And so policy as code
is a pretty simple idea.

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:57.420
It's basically the
ability to take everything

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.400
that you care about
as an administrator,

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.680
codify it, and then start
running your business processes

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:05.790
and your business
rules on top of that.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:09.190
Anthos Config
Management is a tool

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:11.650
designed to help with
that kind of principle.

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:15.860
And so we've got a
multi-cluster architecture.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:17.800
It scales to
hundreds of clusters,

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:20.620
even thousands of clusters,
because the repository is

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:22.600
really the central piece of it.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:25.120
And so anytime you
bring up a new cluster,

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:27.670
if you've got Anthos Config
Management installed on it,

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:29.350
it connects to the
central repository.

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:31.390
It can pull down the
policies that apply

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.010
to that particular cluster.

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:36.110
And that means that we
run fairly distributed.

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:39.430
And so if you have a cluster
in every retail store,

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.250
that's an environment
that we can support.

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:43.877
It works on prem,
and in the cloud.

00:21:43.877 --> 00:21:45.460
There's nothing about
the service that

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:48.430
requires you to talk to GCP.

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:51.670
We are going to
build ways of pulling

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:55.400
in telemetry, ways of
managing it through the cloud.

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.630
But it can run entirely
disconnected on prem.

00:21:57.630 --> 00:22:00.043
So you can keep your
repository on prem.

00:22:00.043 --> 00:22:01.460
You can have your
clusters on prem

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:03.310
if you're running with Anthos.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:05.560
You don't necessarily have
to talk back to the cloud

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:09.070
to get in touch with
your repo or pull down

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:10.660
your configurations.

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:12.290
It is declarative
and continuous.

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:13.690
So by declarative,
what I mean is

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:15.820
you put your policies
in there as you

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:18.130
expect them to be applied.

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:20.170
You don't have to wait
for them to be applied.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:21.795
You just tell the
system what you want,

00:22:21.795 --> 00:22:23.640
and that's kind of
the Kubernetes way.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.750
And then we take
that declaration.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:29.140
We apply it to all the
different API servers,

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:30.580
and then run in
a continuous loop

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.430
to ensure that the system is
brought in line with that.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:37.710
So that reconciliation
is a constant process.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.520
And what it allows you
to do is define once

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:42.370
how things should be,
and then hand that off

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:45.130
to the controllers that are
running in the various clusters

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.710
to ensure that things
are happening that way.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:49.810
And then migrating to
it is fairly simple

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:52.510
because we are using the
same format for these policy

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:54.565
definitions as Kubernetes.

00:22:54.565 --> 00:22:56.140
It is an open format.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:58.660
It's the format that people
are used to managing clusters.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.035
If you're managing
clusters today

00:23:00.035 --> 00:23:02.170
or you're managing
Istio today, you

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:05.350
can take your configurations,
cut and paste them in,

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:08.900
and it should work.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:12.680
How that looks is that we just
use Git as a source of truth.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:17.150
And so our repository is more
or less a set of YAML files

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:20.030
that are expressing
all of your policies

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:24.530
with a little bit of
massaging to ensure

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:27.800
that the policies that need
to go to one place or another

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.400
are handled.

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:33.200
And so it handles all your
Kubernetes and Istio resources,

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.330
integrates with
your source control.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:37.190
Everything is native YAML.

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:44.450
And we can plug our validators
into whatever source control

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:48.590
system you're using and run
inside the same workflow

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.190
that you have for your code.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.060
It's all meant to be
plugable and extensible.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:56.420
So you can see on the right
here I've got a directory

00:23:56.420 --> 00:23:59.450
structure of a repository.

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:02.510
I'm putting all of my
stuff in namespaces.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:03.410
I've got quota.

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:05.570
I've got role bindings.

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:07.748
And it looks very
much like a repository

00:24:07.748 --> 00:24:10.040
you might set up for yourself
if you were just applying

00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:11.940
these things [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.360
So I'll get more into that
when we go into the demo.

00:24:14.360 --> 00:24:17.760
But that's the basic
structure of it.

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:22.760
So how that looks inside
of a sample architecture

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:25.790
for a hybrid Anthos
deployment is

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:28.460
that you might have a policy
repository that's sitting

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:30.340
in an on-prem data center.

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:33.080
And you can put all of
your Kubernetes definitions

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:35.090
that you need as an
administrator there.

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:38.180
So things like access control,
things like network policy,

00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:40.800
pod security policy, et cetera.

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:43.900
Things like your Istio
mesh enforcing mutual TLS.

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:47.490
That can all go into
that policy store.

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:52.200
And then you may be running
clusters in the Google Cloud

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:53.360
Platform.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:55.640
You may be running
clusters on prem.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.000
As long as you have
interconnect setup

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:00.890
so that that looks like
one routable network,

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:03.932
everything can come back and
talk to the policy repository.

00:25:03.932 --> 00:25:05.390
And all of the
clusters that you've

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:09.380
got running in both
environments are

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:12.800
able to see those and
pull their stuff down.

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:16.430
If that interconnect goes
down, we keep the last state.

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:18.890
We continue to enforce
those policies.

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:22.250
And so any network interruption
between those environments

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:25.010
just means that new changes
aren't able to come across.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:28.490
But the last good
state is still there,

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:30.710
and is still being enforced.

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:32.600
And you can see we have
our config management

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:34.470
operator in both places.

00:25:34.470 --> 00:25:36.860
That's the distributed
system that's

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:38.690
running on those
clusters and making sure

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:40.400
that everything is in line.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:43.160
And the fact that it
is a distributed system

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:47.323
means that, again, if
there's a networking outage,

00:25:47.323 --> 00:25:48.740
those processes
are still running.

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:53.630
And so what this
enables you to do

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.420
is have defense in
depth for the policies

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:58.950
that you really care about.

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:02.750
And it's really a shift
left sort of mentality

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:06.290
for administrative policies.

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:08.180
So the flow here
when you're making

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:11.720
changes, once you have stuff
stored in a Git repository,

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:14.990
is that anything you want to
do, you cut a branch for it.

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:17.067
And that's humans
cutting a branch.

00:26:17.067 --> 00:26:18.650
So say you've got
an administrator who

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:21.230
wants to add a new namespace
for a development team.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.350
Cut a branch, add that there,
do a code review on it,

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:25.640
and push it down.

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:29.630
But you can also have
machine processes, algorithms

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:30.930
that are doing the same thing.

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:34.010
So if we can do static analysis
on all of your policies,

00:26:34.010 --> 00:26:36.240
we can make recommendations.

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.270
One of the things
we announced here

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:40.340
is our policy intelligence
stuff that we're

00:26:40.340 --> 00:26:43.890
doing on Google Cloud
that's doing analysis of IM

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:46.130
to look at things
like overgranting.

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:47.900
Once we're able to
see those things,

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:49.900
we can translate them
back into changes.

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:51.980
If we know your
current state and code,

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:53.480
we know how we
think it should be,

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:54.990
we can send you a pull request.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.350
And so there's a
whole set of stuff

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.810
that you can do once you're
able to do static analysis

00:26:59.810 --> 00:27:01.820
on your policies.

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:03.900
We're just getting
started in that area.

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:06.530
But I think it's a
really promising avenue

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:11.120
once we can get folks to
start doing stuff in code.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:14.130
Once you've got those changes,
you can validate them.

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:16.130
And so you can put all
of your business rules--

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:19.370
at least those business rules
that you're able to codify--

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:21.240
into validation.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.570
So we ship with
validation that looks

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:26.180
at syntax and the
correctness of the repository

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:28.790
to ensure that bad
configs, malformed configs,

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:30.860
are not coming down.

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:33.290
But as we continue to
develop on top of this,

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:34.970
we're going to
ship more and more

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:37.880
validation to allow you
to do things like bring

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:39.375
plugable business roles.

00:27:39.375 --> 00:27:41.000
And what that means
is that if somebody

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:43.298
is in violation of your
corporate policies,

00:27:43.298 --> 00:27:45.590
or they're doing something
that they shouldn't be doing

00:27:45.590 --> 00:27:49.250
in terms of changing
network routing or changing

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:52.220
access controls, you can
catch that in the pipeline

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:54.350
before it hits your
environment, before it goes out

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:55.500
to your clusters.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:57.530
And so you can
shift from grabbing

00:27:57.530 --> 00:28:01.310
those things in auditing every
so often to understanding

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:04.070
that this is a bad
config upfront,

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:05.990
and we're not going to
allow you to push that.

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:09.050
Because we're running, say, a
GitHub check on it that lets

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:11.645
us know that this policy's
outside of the guardrails

00:28:11.645 --> 00:28:14.760
that the administrators
have set for it.

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:16.640
That's going through
code review as well.

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:20.060
So teams of administrators
now can collaborate.

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:21.920
You can have two
people who have to turn

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:24.350
the key on major policies.

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:26.070
You can see who reviewed it.

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:26.960
You can make changes.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:29.600
You can do all of that
before it hits the production

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:31.030
environment.

00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:35.090
And then you can also enforce
things at the deployment stage.

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:39.770
So admission control to block
things from the clusters.

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:43.180
You can use the same logic
invalidation against the API.

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:45.550
So even if something comes
through this process,

00:28:45.550 --> 00:28:47.200
you can stop it from
hitting the API,

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:49.660
and stop us from syncing it.

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:52.750
Reconciliation
and auditing still

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:55.798
allow you to guard against
drift on those clusters.

00:28:55.798 --> 00:28:58.090
So if somebody comes in and
makes a local change that's

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:03.690
out of spec, you can come in
and overwrite that immediately.

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:05.570
I said I'd talk about
admission control here.

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:09.130
So another project that we're
working on in open source

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:11.580
is called Gatekeeper.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:13.880
Gatekeeper is an
admission controller

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:18.920
that we're planning on
including and helping

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:23.200
folks manage in concert
with a lot of this stuff.

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:25.575
The key thing about Gatekeeper
as an admission controller

00:29:25.575 --> 00:29:30.470
is that it uses the Open Policy
Agent, which is a CNCF project.

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:35.480
And Open Policy Agent is
effectively a policy evaluation

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:36.740
engine.

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:42.080
It takes a set of policies
expressed in code that anybody

00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:45.630
can write, and will enforce
them wherever it goes.

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:49.320
So what Gatekeeper does is
wrap that in the infrastructure

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.410
it needs to be run as
an admission controller.

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:57.020
And it allows you to define
as a Kubernetes resource

00:29:57.020 --> 00:29:58.910
any policy you want
to be evaluated

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:01.430
against things that are
going to hit the API server.

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:03.980
So an example of that
might be label enforcement

00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:04.640
on deployments.

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:07.160
If you need all of
your deployments

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:10.070
to have particular labels, or
you need them to have a label,

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:12.290
you can say, look, if
you're going to deploy,

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:13.700
and you're going to
deploy this kind of image,

00:30:13.700 --> 00:30:15.410
it needs to have this
particular label,

00:30:15.410 --> 00:30:17.060
or we're not going to accept it.

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:19.980
And those label rules
may be specific to you,

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.190
and you can program
them in there.

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:25.100
So Gatekeeper comes with
a Kubernetes interface

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:27.807
to push standard
rules, but it also

00:30:27.807 --> 00:30:29.390
has a programming
language that allows

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:31.050
you to write those yourself.

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:35.150
So let's jump into a demo here.

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:38.660
I'll show you a little
bit about how we run.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:41.510
Just give me a second
to log into my machine.

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:59.600
OK, can we bring up the demo?

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:01.870
So what I'm going
to do here is show

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:06.040
you just a simple deployment
of an Istio application.

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:07.757
It's called the
Bookinfo application.

00:31:07.757 --> 00:31:09.340
It's the kind of
demo-- it's the thing

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:11.530
you may have seen in a few
places on the floor here.

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:16.228
It's available in the
Istio docs as an example.

00:31:16.228 --> 00:31:18.520
I'm just going to play around
with some of the policies

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:21.100
here to show you what that
looks like in a code repository,

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:23.830
make a couple of changes, and
we'll go look at that live.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:29.110
So what I have here is a
Tmux session on the left

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:31.420
is where I'm going to be
running all my commands.

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:33.910
It's all scripted, but
it's all running live.

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:36.610
On the right, I've got
my local file system.

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:38.320
So I have a copy of
a repository that I'm

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:40.490
hosting up in GitHub there.

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:43.170
And at the bottom, I've
got a couple of watches.

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:47.050
And those watches are on
live clusters running on GKE.

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:49.750
I'm running on two, but
everything I'm doing here you

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:53.637
could run on thousands of
clusters if you wanted to.

00:31:53.637 --> 00:31:55.720
We've got a watch here,
just looking at some pods.

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:57.345
So you'll be able to
see them coming up

00:31:57.345 --> 00:32:00.740
as I'm making changes
in the repository.

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:02.620
So I'm going to set
my Kubectl to look

00:32:02.620 --> 00:32:05.000
at a particular cluster.

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:07.300
And I'm going to bring
in all of the policies

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:10.440
that I need for my application.

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:13.540
So this is the local
file system here.

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:16.780
This is our repository format
for CSP config management--

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:19.480
sorry, Anthos config management.

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:22.920
We've got cluster scoped
resources at the top.

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:24.670
So I've got a pod
security policy in there

00:32:24.670 --> 00:32:28.030
that's ensuring that
my deployments are only

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:30.910
able to do certain things
against the host environment.

00:32:30.910 --> 00:32:33.760
I've got a cluster role there
for service deployers that

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:36.730
allows me to define on a
namespace per namespace level

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:39.423
who should be allowed to
deploy within that namespace.

00:32:39.423 --> 00:32:40.840
So that's something
I can hand out

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:45.160
to individual groups of
users or individual users.

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:47.620
And then I have all my
namespace level resources.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:50.800
So this is where all of the
deployments are defined.

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:52.720
I've got two namespaces here--

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:54.698
miscellaneous and services.

00:32:54.698 --> 00:32:56.740
And then I have an admin
role binding that I want

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:58.577
to apply across both of them.

00:32:58.577 --> 00:33:00.160
And you notice that
admin role binding

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:05.920
sits in the directory structure
above both of those namespaces.

00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:08.200
What we allow in our
repository format

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:09.970
is kind of copy
down inheritance.

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:12.010
So if you have a
resource that you

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:15.040
want to be spread across
multiple namespaces,

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:17.980
we can use the hierarchy
of the directory structure

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:19.873
to inherit that down.

00:33:19.873 --> 00:33:21.790
We can also apply it to
a group of namespaces.

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:23.457
So if you don't have
a hierarchy and you

00:33:23.457 --> 00:33:26.530
want to just use sets of
namespaces to apply policies,

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:28.550
we have a mechanism for that.

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:31.360
In addition, you can apply
policies to individual clusters

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:34.850
or individual sets of clusters
based on label selection.

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:37.420
So this allows you to say,
OK, I have one set of policies

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:38.620
that need to be everywhere.

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.120
Or have some policies
that just need

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:41.537
to be in these
namespaces, or just

00:33:41.537 --> 00:33:45.400
these clusters, or some
combination thereof.

00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:48.970
I've got all my services
for the Bookinfo app

00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:51.760
here in the services namespace.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:56.860
And then I've got a
miscellaneous namespace

00:33:56.860 --> 00:33:59.020
up there that just has
a namespace definition

00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:01.550
and a quota attached
to it right now.

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:02.150
So it's empty.

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.070
There's nothing running there.

00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:08.280
So let's go look at
what we've got here.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:11.103
So I'm going to add
this to my repository.

00:34:11.103 --> 00:34:12.770
You see nothing's
running at the moment.

00:34:12.770 --> 00:34:13.760
This is all local.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:17.090
It's just a set of
policies in my file system.

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:19.690
I'm going to make a commit
up to my Git repository,

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:22.800
and add all of these
files, and push that live.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:26.600
So this is going up to GitHub,
pushing all of those policies

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:29.389
there and updating
the repository.

00:34:29.389 --> 00:34:32.030
And then I've got the
clusters watching that.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:34.190
And you can see
already I've got pods

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:36.139
initializing on cluster two.

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:40.440
Cluster one's a little bit
behind, but it's catching up.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.830
And so now I've got
all of the services

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:48.380
that I need for my microservices
application to run.

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:50.897
I can go take a
look at the cluster.

00:34:50.897 --> 00:34:52.730
So I'm going to go look
at the role bindings

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:55.790
in the miscellaneous namespace.

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:58.490
So as you can see, there's no
role binding applied directly

00:34:58.490 --> 00:34:59.720
to that namespace.

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:01.370
All it's getting is
an inherited role

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:05.750
binding from the
directory above it.

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:07.570
But if you look at it
live in the cluster,

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:11.320
there's that admin role binding
that showed up 35 seconds ago.

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:14.290
And so this is great for
things like administrative role

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:15.620
bindings, this inheritance.

00:35:15.620 --> 00:35:18.730
And then you can set that once
and apply it and make sure

00:35:18.730 --> 00:35:20.530
that every single one
of your namespaces

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:24.370
in a multi-tenant cluster has
access for your administrators.

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:27.670
It's not just for role binding,
so you can also do things

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:30.020
like apply a network policy.

00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:33.670
So if you have the requirement
that you want everybody

00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:37.145
to be strictly defining
pod-to-pod traffic,

00:35:37.145 --> 00:35:38.770
so you say this
service is only allowed

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:41.560
to talk to that service, you
could put an empty network

00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:44.240
policy at the root
of your repository

00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:46.810
and have it inherited down
to all of your namespaces.

00:35:46.810 --> 00:35:48.280
What that will do
is, by default,

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:51.465
cut off all traffic
outside of the namespace.

00:35:51.465 --> 00:35:52.840
And it will force
your developers

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:56.182
to come in and define it
explicitly on top of that.

00:35:56.182 --> 00:35:57.640
So it's different
ways that you can

00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:00.910
use that to simplify
your policy application,

00:36:00.910 --> 00:36:02.560
not have to be
repetitive and doing

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:04.494
things namespace by namespace.

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:09.080
One of the other
nice things we do

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:12.020
is guard against people coming
in and doing destructive things

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:13.890
against administrative policies.

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:16.400
So that's in admin role binding.

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:18.720
If I'm an administrator, I
probably want that there.

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:20.482
I need to be able
to get in there.

00:36:20.482 --> 00:36:22.190
If somebody came in
and decided that they

00:36:22.190 --> 00:36:24.200
were going to delete
my SRE admin role

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:27.920
binding from their namespace so
they could lock out the admins,

00:36:27.920 --> 00:36:29.392
that would be bad.

00:36:29.392 --> 00:36:31.350
I've got administrative
access on this cluster,

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:33.608
so I can go do that.

00:36:33.608 --> 00:36:35.150
So let's go do
something destructive.

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:38.490
Let's delete that role binding.

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:41.430
So now my SREs
can't get back in.

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:45.380
But if we go look at it on the
cluster, you can see it's back.

00:36:45.380 --> 00:36:48.890
Seven seconds ago, which is
just about when I applied it,

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:52.550
we came back in and rewrote it.

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:54.380
And so if you come
in and you try

00:36:54.380 --> 00:36:57.530
to affect one of these policies
that the administrator has

00:36:57.530 --> 00:37:00.410
applied, we can
automatically reconcile

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:04.840
that back to whatever's
in the repository.

00:37:04.840 --> 00:37:13.270
So let's go look at the quota
in the miscellaneous namespace.

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:15.670
So this is resource quota
that I'm applying here.

00:37:15.670 --> 00:37:18.000
So in this namespace,
I put in a policy that

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:19.620
says you can only have one pod.

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:22.850
You can only use 10%
of a virtual CPU.

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:25.410
It only gets 100 megs of memory.

00:37:25.410 --> 00:37:28.110
If I want to say,
deploy a pod here,

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:31.600
I can do that as long as
I'm within that quota.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:33.730
I'm going to deploy--

00:37:33.730 --> 00:37:35.710
it's kind of cut off
on the left there,

00:37:35.710 --> 00:37:41.290
but it is a instance,
a BusyBox that sleeps.

00:37:41.290 --> 00:37:43.030
This is not a
particularly useful thing

00:37:43.030 --> 00:37:46.780
to deploy in your clusters,
but just for an example.

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:50.590
You can notice, if you
see just at the top there,

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:53.000
I'm deploying a pod, but I
happen to have misspelled it.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:56.350
It says Pd instead of
Pod up on the left.

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:58.900
So this won't work if I try
to push it to the cluster.

00:37:58.900 --> 00:38:02.230
If I put it in the repository,
it'll never get synced down.

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:04.040
I'm just writing it locally.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:05.950
So that's not a problem yet.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:09.080
As I said, one of the things
we ship with is a validation.

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:11.620
So we have a command
line tool called Nomos.

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:13.690
This can run is a Git hook.

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:20.650
It can run as a check, say,
in GitHub or some other source

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:23.560
tooling system so that every
time you push something

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:25.120
into the repository,
you can validate

00:38:25.120 --> 00:38:28.217
that it's syntactically
correct, that it's correct

00:38:28.217 --> 00:38:29.800
based on the format
of the repository,

00:38:29.800 --> 00:38:32.680
and that you're not going to
be trying to push bad configs.

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:36.990
If I run that against this pod
definition, it throws an error.

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:43.340
It lets me know no matches
for Kind Pd in version v1.

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:46.365
So let's go fix that.

00:38:46.365 --> 00:38:51.090
I'm going to come in here
and add my missing o,

00:38:51.090 --> 00:38:52.010
and then save it.

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:57.490
And so if I run that
validation again,

00:38:57.490 --> 00:38:59.390
it passes clean with no errors.

00:38:59.390 --> 00:39:01.930
So now I'm ready to go.

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:03.370
And that's a clean pod.

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:08.110
So let's go take a look at
the running services here.

00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:10.690
I'm going to figure out
where my service is actually

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:14.070
running here by looking
at the gateway to Istio.

00:39:16.810 --> 00:39:19.428
I'm going to pick this up,
and we can go look at it.

00:39:19.428 --> 00:39:21.220
You see this is the
GitHub repository where

00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:23.910
I'm running right now.

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:26.470
If I refresh that, you can
see all of the resources

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:27.862
that I've got here.

00:39:27.862 --> 00:39:29.445
So let's go look at
our Istio service.

00:39:33.668 --> 00:39:34.960
Doesn't look like it's running.

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:40.120
Seems like I've got a
networking problem here.

00:39:44.690 --> 00:39:45.670
Try one more thing.

00:39:51.970 --> 00:39:53.850
So it looks like my
pods are running,

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:56.745
but it is not routable.

00:39:56.745 --> 00:40:00.300
Not sure if that's my local
networking causing problems

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:01.050
or not.

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:03.322
All right, well, we'll move on.

00:40:03.322 --> 00:40:04.280
We've got running pods.

00:40:04.280 --> 00:40:07.430
We've got no network
connectivity.

00:40:07.430 --> 00:40:10.630
So one of the things
I can do with this

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:15.700
is change some of the routing
rules in my microservices mesh.

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:18.120
So the Istio allows
you to define

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:19.990
what we call virtual services.

00:40:19.990 --> 00:40:21.700
Virtual services
can take traffic

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.260
that's coming in
through the gateway

00:40:23.260 --> 00:40:25.960
and route it into
individual pods.

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:28.980
I've got virtual services
defined here for various things

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:33.140
that are running in my
microservices architecture.

00:40:33.140 --> 00:40:38.290
And if you look, you can see
I've got a review service here

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:39.940
that's routing
100% of my traffic

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:42.910
to the v1 pod of reviews.

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:45.880
There are actually
three different versions

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:48.380
of reviews running on
each of these clusters.

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:51.490
So if I want, I can
do an upgrade in place

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:53.770
by routing the
traffic from v1 to v2,

00:40:53.770 --> 00:40:55.300
because those are running.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:57.160
Now, in a real life
environment, I probably

00:40:57.160 --> 00:40:59.420
would do this very slowly.

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:01.810
I would take it
and I would maybe

00:41:01.810 --> 00:41:03.520
have a canary branch
write a cluster that

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:05.920
was pointing at a canary
branch of my policies.

00:41:05.920 --> 00:41:07.960
Make that change in
the canary branch.

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:09.400
Go take a look at it live.

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:11.920
Make sure it was working.

00:41:11.920 --> 00:41:15.350
I may decide to route just
a very small percentage

00:41:15.350 --> 00:41:18.790
of traffic there, go
look at my monitoring,

00:41:18.790 --> 00:41:20.290
and make sure that
that was working.

00:41:20.290 --> 00:41:23.050
You can do all of that
directly through this kind

00:41:23.050 --> 00:41:24.370
of code-based interface.

00:41:24.370 --> 00:41:27.220
But since we're
short on time, let's

00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:29.870
just do it live in production.

00:41:29.870 --> 00:41:32.010
So I'm going to
upgrade this to v2.

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:35.960
And I'm going to save it.

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:38.800
And that's all I need to
do to upgrade that service.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:40.630
Now, what that
does is it changes

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:44.740
to a new version of reviews
that actually calls the ratings

00:41:44.740 --> 00:41:45.400
system.

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:48.100
So it's going to bring
in additional information

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:49.990
to the Bookinfo app.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:55.460
And we can make that
change and push it up.

00:41:59.897 --> 00:42:01.870
OK.

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:03.790
So that's gone out to GitHub.

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:06.060
And now if we look
at the live cluster,

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:08.452
it's still looking at v1,
but it just flipped over.

00:42:08.452 --> 00:42:10.410
So you can see where it
says subset down there.

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:12.353
Now it's pointing at v2.

00:42:12.353 --> 00:42:13.770
So we've changed
our routing rules

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:14.937
live on all of our clusters.

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:21.970
If I go look at
this other cluster,

00:42:21.970 --> 00:42:24.280
hopefully my service
came up there.

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:27.360
So let's see that one
and see if it's live.

00:42:35.570 --> 00:42:37.760
OK, one out of two ain't bad.

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:39.860
So this is my other cluster.

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:42.650
If you had seen it before when
it was working on cluster 1,

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:45.070
you would have seen that
there were no star ratings.

00:42:45.070 --> 00:42:46.695
There are star ratings
now because I've

00:42:46.695 --> 00:42:49.370
upgraded that version
of the microservice.

00:42:49.370 --> 00:42:53.480
And it is pulling from
the rating service

00:42:53.480 --> 00:42:55.432
because of the routing
rules I've changed.

00:42:55.432 --> 00:42:57.890
And these routing rules have
changed on both those clusters

00:42:57.890 --> 00:42:59.880
simultaneously.

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:02.960
If I were to enroll 10
clusters or 20 clusters,

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:04.850
I could make that change
across all of them.

00:43:04.850 --> 00:43:07.340
Or I could make that change
across a subset of them,

00:43:07.340 --> 00:43:09.050
depending on what
I want to do there.

00:43:13.910 --> 00:43:18.470
One other good thing about
having a Git repository

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:19.640
is that it's transactional.

00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:21.350
So everything that
I've done here

00:43:21.350 --> 00:43:23.100
was done in a particular commit.

00:43:23.100 --> 00:43:25.250
And I can roll those
back in any time.

00:43:25.250 --> 00:43:27.290
If I want to undo
all of my changes

00:43:27.290 --> 00:43:30.230
and get back to a clean
state that I know is OK,

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:32.720
I can just delete
everything I added locally

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:36.670
in my environment, and then
push it back up to the cluster.

00:43:36.670 --> 00:43:38.150
And so effectively
what that does

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:41.358
is reverse all of the
commits that I made,

00:43:41.358 --> 00:43:43.400
removes all the files that
I added, and goes back

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:46.530
to the state that we were
at when we started the demo.

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:48.830
And if I push that
up, it's going

00:43:48.830 --> 00:43:50.680
to clean everything
out that I built.

00:43:50.680 --> 00:43:52.250
It's going to clean out
all of those routing rules.

00:43:52.250 --> 00:43:53.840
Is going to clean
out all these pods.

00:43:53.840 --> 00:43:58.110
It's going to restore my state
back to where I wanted it.

00:43:58.110 --> 00:44:03.280
And if we look, we should see
those pods start to terminate.

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:04.590
And there they go.

00:44:04.590 --> 00:44:06.653
And so this is going to
roll everything back,

00:44:06.653 --> 00:44:09.070
bring it back to an entirely
clean slate where I've gotten

00:44:09.070 --> 00:44:11.110
naked clusters.

00:44:11.110 --> 00:44:12.527
So that is my demo.

00:44:12.527 --> 00:44:14.110
Can you go back to
the slides, please?

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:26.170
So that's CSP
Config Management--

00:44:26.170 --> 00:44:27.520
Anthos Config Management.

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:30.220
I'll get used to
that eventually.

00:44:30.220 --> 00:44:32.050
If you're interested,
it's available now

00:44:32.050 --> 00:44:33.440
to Anthos customers.

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:37.000
So if were a participating
in the Anthos beta

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:39.960
or you're signing up for Anthos,
this comes along with it.

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:41.050
It works on prem today.

00:44:41.050 --> 00:44:42.757
It works in the cloud today.

00:44:42.757 --> 00:44:44.590
If you're interested
in getting early access

00:44:44.590 --> 00:44:46.600
because you're a
GKE customer, we

00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:49.430
are going to make it
available to GKE customers.

00:44:49.430 --> 00:44:52.058
And so you can sign up here
if you're interested in that.

00:44:52.058 --> 00:44:54.100
And I'll whitelist you
for that and bring you in.

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:56.890
We do expect to make it
available on GKE as well

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:58.720
for folks who are
purely cloud based

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:02.070
and working with us in GCP.

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:03.570
We're out of time.

00:45:03.570 --> 00:45:05.770
I'll hang out if
folks want to talk.

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:07.950
Thank you very much.

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:11.900
